{"v":1,"st":"NC","y":"2020","L":{"cd":{"f":"","n":0,"lbl":"U.S. House","src":[["U.S. Census Bureau, cartographic boundary file cb_2020_us_cd116_500k","https://www2.census.gov/geo/tiger/GENZ2020/shp/cb_2020_us_cd116_500k.zip","35113ca7612b14333bfbf24ec074d01278bc8ea13d836213b1c7390ba945c830"]],"vint":"The Census Bureau's lines of the 116th Congress's districts (its file says session 116). The Bureau published no file of the 117th Congress's districts, the Congress the 2020 election chose.","chk":[7,13,6],"why":"No map of North Carolina's U.S. House districts for 2020: the election was held on the 2019 congressional plan (H.B. 1029, S.L. 2019-249, enacted Nov. 15, 2019), and the Census Bureau published no file of those lines, so this page draws none. The official record of the plan is under The lines.","rec":["2019 congressional plan (H.B. 1029, S.L. 2019-249, enacted Nov. 15, 2019)","2019-11-15","N.C. General Assembly, Legislative and Congressional Redistricting (plans, session laws, dates and the election each was used for)","https://ncleg.gov/Redistricting","Enacted after the Wake County three-judge panel enjoined the 2016 plan (Harper v. Lewis, 19 CVS 012667, Oct. 28, 2019); used for the March 3, 2020 primary and the 2020 general. The Bureau published no 117th Congress file, and its 116th file (the Congress elected in 2018) holds the 2016 plan (S.L. 2016-1), not this one."]},"sldu":{"f":"","n":0,"lbl":"State Senate","src":[["U.S. Census Bureau, cartographic boundary file cb_2020_us_sldu_500k","https://www2.census.gov/geo/tiger/GENZ2020/shp/cb_2020_us_sldu_500k.zip","5fcedd3ac126a22d9beaefda04abf217f27bc29e1f515a7255b1c3b6c13bb594"]],"vint":"The Census Bureau's 2020 file of legislative districts, the lines it records for the 2018 legislative session year. The Bureau published no file of the lines in use for 2020. Where a state's lines changed in between, the check below or the plan cited stops the map.","chk":[50,50,0],"why":"No map of North Carolina's upper chambe